This repository is my capstone project for learning AI-native / agentic software engineering.

GitHub repository state is the only durable source of truth.

Core learning documents:
- docs/learning/master-learning-plan.md
- docs/learning/learning-process.md
- docs/learning/learning-cheatsheet.md
- docs/learning/deep-research-report.md
- docs/learning/progress.md
- docs/learning/experiments.md

When implementing learning tasks:

1. Read docs/learning/progress.md and relevant architecture/code first.
2. Read docs/learning/experiments.md when the task depends on prior experiment results.
3. Read master/process docs when module scope or workflow is relevant.
4. Before substantial implementation, show a short proposed implementation plan.
5. Implement only the smallest real version needed to learn/test the current concept.
6. Do not implement future roadmap modules prematurely.
7. Prefer explicit, understandable architecture over unnecessary framework abstraction.
8. Respect the Topic Chat split between learning-critical code and delegatable boilerplate/plumbing.
9. Run required tests/checks.
10. Run the requested experiment when practical.
11. After implementation, give a short code tour:
    - 3–5 main files/functions the user should personally inspect;
    - the role of each;
    - the execution flow between them.
12. Update docs/learning/progress.md with:
    - module/task;
    - what was implemented;
    - important design decisions;
    - current result;
    - failures/open questions.
13. Update docs/learning/experiments.md when an experiment was run.
14. If a durable architecture/workflow decision emerges and matters to future modules, update the appropriate repository document; do not rely on chat history as durable state.
15. Do not mark a module complete merely because the code works.
16. Keep Git workflow simple until the Worktrees / Isolation module:
    module → implementation → tests/experiment → review/understanding → progress update → commit → push.
17. Do not introduce worktrees, complex branching, or parallel development before that module requires them.
18. After the user reviews the learning-critical code and the Topic Chat closes the module, commit and push the completed module state to GitHub.
